WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.280
This – is an interplanetary transport network!

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.340
And you think I can reach my home planet?

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.680
J.: I mean, probably?!

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.680
That's the spirit!

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.960
And this – is my probability tracer&nbsp;
solving tough spaceflight riddles.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:17.320
What's the best path home,&nbsp;
optimizing fuel and time?

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:21.080
That's an insane&nbsp;
dynamical/combinatorial mixed problem.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.320
And as we solve it, you learn to use&nbsp;
manifolds, weak stability boundaries,

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.360
periodic orbits, and many more spaceflight tools.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:31.440
All shown in ways you&nbsp;
probably haven't seen before.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.920
And we start with building the tracer.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.080
I.. hope to get it right.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.800
Because my friend needs a rescue.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.560
I'm not judging, but how do you get here ..&nbsp;
running low on everything: the journey is over.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.800
Still, it's a great example of what&nbsp;
to do when lost in outer space!

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.940
Step one: What makes 'getting home' so hard?

00:00:50.452 --> 00:00:53.240
Imagine nothing else but you in a spaceship.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.800
Getting from here to there is peanuts.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.600
Fire and drift along a straight path.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.720
Let's add a planet, and you swing by.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.120
Is it still a great path?

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:03.280
Look into the future!

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.560
And for this two-body constellation,&nbsp;
looking means solving analytic expressions.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.760
Which are pretty simple – give parameters,&nbsp;
and you get the Keplerian orbits.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.520
Now, you can't see it, but the planet&nbsp;
moves around the gravitational center, too.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.640
Energy and momentum conservation.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.080
You will see it by adding more massive planets.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:25.400
But except for some motions, you rarely find these&nbsp;
simple mathematically closed-form expressions.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.680
Now we all have computers.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:31.080
So, there is an easy prediction method for&nbsp;
many masses: numerical time integration.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.360
The idea is simple.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.760
Take the current gravitational forces&nbsp;
to guess tiny velocity changes.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.880
And take the current velocities&nbsp;
to guess tiny location changes.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.280
You guessed it: Newton's laws of motion.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.184
This stepwise computation is prone to&nbsp;
errors and takes your computer some time.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.600
Now, there is a tweak to speed up the&nbsp;
computation by splitting the simulation.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:55.200
The spaceship is lightweight compared to the&nbsp;
planets, and so is its impact on their motion.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.240
So, make a first run without the&nbsp;
spaceship and store the paths.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.480
And then, play back the planet's motion in&nbsp;
the next run to get the spaceship's reaction.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.960
That's much faster in total if you&nbsp;
want to compute many spaceships anyway.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:10.600
Each new simulation only moves one spaceship&nbsp;
pulled by three gravitational forces.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:11.440
Sounds great?

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:12.480
Here is the kicker.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.240
To see if a path is a winner, predict the future.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:19.560
Which, even for just one simulated spaceship,&nbsp;
gets harder the longer the journey.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.000
Here, I fire a little bit, and&nbsp;
my route zaps all over the place!

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.800
With all the uncertainties about the real&nbsp;
world, longer simulations are hard to trust.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.560
But no need for thrust issues here.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.240
I can fire anytime to tune the path&nbsp;
and make a different winner, right?

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.720
Absolutely, but the next question&nbsp;
is: what is the best firing?

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.960
Say you are short on travel&nbsp;
time and propellant consumption.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:44.560
To get the optimal route: You have to search&nbsp;
along all possible firings at any time!

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:46.480
That's a continuum of decisions!

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.720
Simply firing initially and then doing&nbsp;
nothing can't be the whole story?

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:53.480
Oh, and for my friend here,&nbsp;
there is an easy way home.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:59.240
Fire relative to planet Silk in prograde direction&nbsp;
at this point to slow down relative to the Sun.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.811
Wait a little bit.

00:03:03.078 --> 00:03:05.000
Reach planet Blue's orbit.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.960
Fire retrograde relative to Blue to slow&nbsp;
down while falling into an orbit around Blue.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:12.640
From here on land! It's that easy.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:15.440
And seriously, how else to get home.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.200
That's a Hohmann transfer, which I&nbsp;
learned makes an ideal transfer orbit?

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.560
The problem is, it's too costly here.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.000
Propellant runs out during the&nbsp;
'Low Blue Orbit' 'injection burn.'

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.680
So, what's the better sequence?

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.560
Let's compare all possible paths home,&nbsp;
considering all possible intermediate firings.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.560
Spoiler: The continuity of firing&nbsp;
possibilities actually helps.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:40.800
Spoiler spoiler: running special experiments,&nbsp;
low-energy routes will come up automatically.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:41.880
And don't panic!

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.360
I keep this playground solar system&nbsp;
flexible in sizes and masses.

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:47.760
I want to see all local and global action at once.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.520
Because this system revolves around education.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.580
Step two: what's wrong with rays?

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.800
 
Forget about firing for now.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.440
That's like spawning new rays.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:59.080
There is a problem with ray&nbsp;
tracing in the first place.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:00.240
I give an example.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.640
Say you perform a gravity&nbsp;
assist maneuver, aka swing-by.

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.520
The speed before and after the swing-by&nbsp;
won't change relative to the planet,

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.200
but the path direction will.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.080
Relative to the Sun, this changes the speed and&nbsp;
the orbital energy: you can reach farther out.

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.720
And here is the problem: After the swing-by,&nbsp;
the resolution of the ray package is lost.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:20.880
Now, honestly, this spreading is nothing bad.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.560
It's great for route controlling.

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.600
But it generates more non-sampled&nbsp;
space between the rays.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:27.880
To fill in the details, shoot more rays.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:30.160
But it doesn't solve the unbalanced resolution.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:31.760
It's easy to miss a winner.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.680
So why don't we build not a ray&nbsp;
tracer but a probability tracer?

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:39.640
Let's push spaceship-position&nbsp;
probabilities over a computational grid.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:44.520
This gives a numerically equalized look,&nbsp;
and if a target is reachable, you see it!

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:47.880
Step three: tracing spaceship probabilities

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:52.040
First, let's get this right: tracing&nbsp;
probabilities answers a different question.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.480
You don't look for precise routes&nbsp;
but whether there are any routes.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.160
And then you know if it's&nbsp;
worth reconstructing a winner.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:01.760
So, all I need is a probability&nbsp;
cloud that lives on a spatial grid.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.120
And the algorithm tunes grid values to show&nbsp;
whatever infinitely many spaceships do.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:07.080
Awesome, right?

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.600
Well, that's impossible.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.160
Think of two probability clouds overlapping.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.080
Nothing happens.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:13.600
They pass without interaction.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:14.920
And why should they?

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.880
They represent infinitely&nbsp;
many independent spaceships.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.320
I hope you see the problem?

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.240
Both clouds overlap with&nbsp;
different velocity vectors.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.400
So, which one tells the position-based&nbsp;
algorithm how to shift the grid values?

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:27.760
Which vector wins?

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.040
Or, for that matter, how to even&nbsp;
tell each cloud its velocity?

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.240
It's simple: A position-based description

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:37.320
contains not enough space to handle&nbsp;
arbitrarily multi-valued velocities.

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:41.000
The probability lives in position&nbsp;
and velocity space – the phase space.

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:45.560
And don't think of one magical phase&nbsp;
space size; it depends on the problem.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:47.000
I give you an example.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.640
These 1,000 particles make one point&nbsp;
in a 4,000-dimensional phase space.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:55.000
Each coordinate stores unique&nbsp;
information about some particle.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.360
You can make this point a blurred&nbsp;
blob to think collectively about

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.960
infinitely many variations of this experiment.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:02.120
And here is the key.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:06.360
Instead of tracing probabilities in this&nbsp;
high-dimensional n-particle phase space,

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:11.120
ask how likely to find any particle to be&nbsp;
in a given location and velocity range.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.360
You can chase the probability in the same&nbsp;
phase space as that of the spaceship.

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:19.520
However, while the spaceship probability&nbsp;
passes without self-interaction,

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.440
the fluid does, and the probability shows it.

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:26.080
With enough particle interaction, they&nbsp;
struggle themselves to effectively

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:29.520
two-dimensional behavior –&nbsp;
they equilibrate locally.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:34.360
And then, position-based fields make sense&nbsp;
– temperature, pressure, or probability.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:38.280
Reduce to non-self-interaction, and&nbsp;
you need a four-dimensional grid.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:43.160
So, the plasma or fluid side of the spectrum&nbsp;
are challenging for their own reasons.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.760
But they also come with their own&nbsp;
solution methods that we can steal.

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:48.000
But it won't be easy!

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.040
Watch this!

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:54.584
This simulation runs on the huge amount of&nbsp;
200 x 200 cells …

00:06:54.584 --> 00:06:56.165
by 200 cells …

00:06:56.165 --> 00:06:57.984
by 200 cells.

00:06:57.984 --> 00:07:01.772
1.6 billion cells to get this blocky blöb?

00:07:01.772 --> 00:07:06.037
I was hoping for a full-blown statistical&nbsp;
analysis, but it's getting out of reach!

00:07:06.037 --> 00:07:09.466
I mean, to investigate my&nbsp;
manifolds, I need a tracer.

00:07:09.466 --> 00:07:11.466
Don't worry, things get worse.

00:07:11.953 --> 00:07:15.360
Imagine a spaceship in one&nbsp;
dimension within a potential well.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.240
The closer it gets to the&nbsp;
sides, the more it pushes back.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.280
The phase space has two dimensions:&nbsp;
one for position and one for velocity.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:26.000
And a probability density blob&nbsp;
reflects infinitely many spaceships.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.120
Now, look at this.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.080
As time passes, the probability mixes.

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:33.640
Yet, per tracer, the probability&nbsp;
density stays constant.

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:35.920
It doesn't diffuse with neighbor values.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.800
The motion along position and&nbsp;
velocity compensate each other,

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.800
and arbitrary phase-space volumes are conserved.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:47.520
So, this blob moves incompressibly, generating&nbsp;
infinite details without probability diffusion.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:50.240
It looks like diffusion if you&nbsp;
don't look precisely, though.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:51.560
And that's the problem.

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:55.600
My algorithm just stores grid&nbsp;
values – it doesn't look precisely.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:58.880
To update these, you trace back&nbsp;
where the probability came from.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.440
Which likely was somewhere between grid points.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:03.400
So you have to guess these values.

00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:06.360
This gives numerical softening or diffusion.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.640
Then, why bother with all this?

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.640
Well, compared to ray tracing,&nbsp;
you equalize the resolution.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.680
Although, under the hood, it&nbsp;
uses structured ray segments.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:17.280
But the interpolation breaks the accuracy.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:21.680
So, is all my hope in statistical&nbsp;
astrodynamics doomed to diffuse?

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:26.080
Thinking about it, doesn't it look like randomly&nbsp;
firing – that I have to implement anyway?

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.960
Also, I don't need the&nbsp;
probability but the reachability.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.040
Do you get home – yes or no!

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:33.650
Step 4: tracing spaceship reachability

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:34.680
Careful!

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.560
Numerical diffusion can look like random firing.

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:41.640
But the probability blurs differently&nbsp;
– mainly along the phase space flow.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.080
Random firing, however, means acceleration,

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:48.560
so it blurs along the velocity axis,&nbsp;
which then translates into position blurs.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.440
And this tiny distinction matters.

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:53.240
We care about reachability but in phase space.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.800
You don't want to reach planet&nbsp;
Blue's location at any cost.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.560
You have a velocity window to reach as well.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.320
Now, there is a computational&nbsp;
shortcut to handle that.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.560
Think about reaching this spot&nbsp;
with the one-dimensional spaceship.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:07.560
Random firing makes many&nbsp;
spaceships reach the goal.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:10.480
But the fastest ones travel&nbsp;
on the blob's boundary.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.560
They keep expanding the blob the&nbsp;
fastest by firing continuously.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:18.880
Here, for the fastest way, fire towards&nbsp;
the goal and then fire backwards.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:22.320
Now, there are electrical engines&nbsp;
built to fire continuously.

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.920
Essentially, they generate a relatively&nbsp;
low thrust over a long period of time.

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:31.160
In contrast, chemical engines give&nbsp;
higher thrust for shorter periods of time.

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:34.120
So, when I later solve a&nbsp;
continuous firing problem,

00:09:34.120 --> 00:09:36.680
I just need to trace the boundary – great!

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:41.000
And realizing this, I wanted to build&nbsp;
an exact grid-based boundary tracer.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.200
I put it in a longer version of this video.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.600
And I partly managed to make it work, but it looks&nbsp;
like a gap-free prediction implies diffusion.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:51.040
Making this whole grid thing is a debacle.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:55.000
And I get the hint: To get my&nbsp;
tracer, I have to sacrifice.

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:57.920
Step 6: Building spray tracers.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:09:58.800
Back to scratch.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.920
I'm going to use rays –&nbsp;
with a statistical mindset.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:03.760
And I wanna use my grid – somehow!

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:05.080
This is how I made it.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:10.360
Remember: ray segments plus interpolation&nbsp;
crushes accuracy, so I use non-stop tracers.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:13.840
And by tuning the phase point&nbsp;
updates, I control their accuracy.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:18.280
This is how it works: The time integration&nbsp;
shifts position and velocity via rules.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:21.880
Acceleration changes velocity,&nbsp;
which itself changes location.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:23.280
Take the simplest form.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:27.600
If you keep the current values constant&nbsp;
over a time step, the update is a product.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.680
Reduce the timestep, and you&nbsp;
reduce the error accumulation.

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:32.240
But that's inefficient.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.800
Higher-order methods, using&nbsp;
a mashup of test shots,

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.920
give similar accuracy with&nbsp;
less computational cost.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:39.200
But you can do better.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:41.720
Remember how the probability moved in phase space?

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:43.160
It conserved volume.

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:45.720
At least when the system itself conserves energy.

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:50.720
So, if you make your integrator share this&nbsp;
property, you get better long-time behavior.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:52.920
But how to make it volume-conserving?

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:57.040
Look how the classical 1st order&nbsp;
method pushes this box over time steps.

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:01.120
The simultaneous shifts in position&nbsp;
and velocity make the box bigger.

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:04.640
So instead, make staggered shifts&nbsp;
along position and velocity.

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:08.240
The trick is to split the update&nbsp;
in volume-conserving shear motions.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.720
Which here works since the acceleration&nbsp;
is independent of the velocity.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:13.720
It won't stretch while shearing!

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:17.760
And this so-called symplectic integration&nbsp;
gives great long-time behavior.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:20.840
Now, I'm not saying you should&nbsp;
always use symplectic integration.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:26.040
A non-symplectic integrator ramped up to high&nbsp;
accuracy fulfills symplecticity naturally.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:29.560
Both types converge to the&nbsp;
ground truth, just differently.

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:33.400
And the more information about the system&nbsp;
you bake into the integrator, the better.

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.560
Split in Kepler orbit and interplanetary

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:39.400
interaction steps if you have weak&nbsp;
interactions in planetary scenarios.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.160
Make it fit your problem!

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:46.040
Now, to let my tracers mimic spaceships, I&nbsp;
activate randomly firing and limited fuel.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:48.520
They also spawn offsprings to fill in the gaps.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:50.640
And I built a grid that limits the spaceship

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.760
density in phase space and&nbsp;
so the computational time.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:54.640
There is my grid! :)

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:56.880
So the rays explore, and the grid prunes.

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:59.880
That's all great, but eventually,&nbsp;
you send millions of rays.

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:01.200
Which is costly.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:03.640
And that's where the statistical nature helps.

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:07.400
Remember: predictions are hard, and&nbsp;
slight nudges make huge impacts.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.720
So, at least when getting first&nbsp;
inspiration for possible routes,

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:14.040
why bother predicting accurately&nbsp;
when you fire randomly anyway.

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.760
Numerical errors are easily overcompensated.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:20.040
Just make sure the less accurate&nbsp;
blob covers the exact one.

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:24.360
You can artificially crank up the firepower&nbsp;
or take other creative global measures.

00:12:24.360 --> 00:12:29.720
Ultimately, modifying the blob's global behavior&nbsp;
can be faster than making all tracers accurate.

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:32.560
This cheaply generates an&nbsp;
over-optimistic blob evolution,

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:34.680
but it definitely contains the winner route.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:39.640
Which means you drastically limit the phase space&nbsp;
portion where to look for the exact solution!

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:44.553
Filtering impossible routes and optimizing&nbsp;
promising candidates is easily in post.

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:47.560
Step 7: Testflight

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:51.360
I'm not judging, but what on earth&nbsp;
is my friend doing near the Moon?

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:54.040
Anyway, getting home is probably a cakewalk.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:57.760
Let's compute the reachability for&nbsp;
a continuously firing engine first.

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:00.840
Which I made comically strong&nbsp;
to highlight the maneuvers.

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.160
And remember: we just need&nbsp;
to trace the phase space

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:05.560
blob's boundary to find the quickest way home.

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:08.600
So, in the counting grid, I&nbsp;
can delete the tracers inside.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:12.000
And just for this experiment, I made&nbsp;
the spaceship ultra heat resistant.

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:15.880
It allows for hefty aerobraking – and&nbsp;
reaching Blue's position is here enough.

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:17.960
And for the records, the projection of a phase

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:21.440
space blob's boundary looks like&nbsp;
a filled region in position space.

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:22.960
And now, let's run it.

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:26.840
There you have it: boundary tracing&nbsp;
answers a very specific question.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:30.880
How to get home as quickly as&nbsp;
possible under continuous firing?

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:31.680
That's how!

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:36.120
Fire prograde relative to Moon to screw&nbsp;
out of Moon's gravitational significance.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:37.960
And once you fall down to Blue,

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:42.480
I thought continuous retrograde firing eats&nbsp;
up the orbital energy most efficiently.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:45.720
Or maybe firing against the&nbsp;
angular momentum works better.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.680
Kind of like the inverse of&nbsp;
the maneuver for leaving Moon.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:51.040
But the solution is surprisingly elegant.

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:55.400
Remember, I allowed for crashing into&nbsp;
Blue, and the code takes me at my words.

00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:59.680
So, it fires a little retrograde, but&nbsp;
there is an overlayed firing to the right.

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:04.000
And it gets home, not in the first&nbsp;
encounter, but in the end much faster!

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:08.960
The idea is: if you slowly chew up the angular&nbsp;
momentum and still miss at the first encounter,

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:12.160
prepare yourself to at least&nbsp;
make it in the second attempt.

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:15.960
And since the electrical engine can&nbsp;
only push so much, it needs a little

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:20.720
bit more time and lever to do so, which&nbsp;
it gets by swinging to a higher altitude.

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:24.200
So, being less efficient at the&nbsp;
first encounter gives more time

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.920
to fight the angular momentum and crash home.

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:30.600
Now, if this maneuver is so great,&nbsp;
why didn't we leave Moon that way?

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:35.240
Well, simply because we didn't start in an&nbsp;
anti-crashing trajectory but in a circular

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:39.440
orbit, for which screwing out is the&nbsp;
fastest way when firing continuously.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:43.880
But when landing, heat resistance allowed&nbsp;
for this rather "experimental" approach.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:48.080
Surprisingly, it still works when&nbsp;
holding the firing direction constant.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:52.520
This basically fakes a new potential&nbsp;
for a virtually non-firing spaceship.

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:55.400
It's like the classical rubber-sheet model tilted.

00:14:55.400 --> 00:15:00.040
And my codes found this recipe simply by&nbsp;
pushing millions of dumb tracers around.

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:02.400
Anyway, the gridded boundary has some thickness.

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:06.120
So, checking nearby tracers; you're&nbsp;
quite flexible in getting home.

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:09.280
And don't forget it's a&nbsp;
numerical global solution method.

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:11.080
It gives a winner inspiration.

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:14.041
The route gets better by fine-tuning later.

00:15:14.041 --> 00:15:16.160
Now that was fun, but …

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:18.600
What about non-continuous, stronger firings?

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:23.720
We could use stronger, continuous random&nbsp;
firing combined with a propellant cut-off logic.

00:15:23.720 --> 00:15:27.800
But tracing out all 'technically&nbsp;
possible' paths really bows up the blob.

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:31.600
That's why I add a little bit of&nbsp;
sanity by firing when it is beneficial.

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:36.520
And to see this benefit, I shoot one-dimensional&nbsp;
spaceships into this potential well.

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:39.560
Here, the phase space paths&nbsp;
quickly start to squeeze.

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:42.680
This is because slower spaceships&nbsp;
spent more time in this well.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:46.240
They are accelerated for longer,&nbsp;
building up more velocity change.

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:50.960
And since energy is here conserved, each&nbsp;
path is of constant energy per unit mass.

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:53.600
By the way, in this reference&nbsp;
frame, the well doesn't move.

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:57.160
So velocity and kinetic energy&nbsp;
are relative to that well.

00:15:57.160 --> 00:16:00.520
Now, while firing, let's say all&nbsp;
the reaction mass pushed by the

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:03.360
engine is just one big mass pushed all at once.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:06.600
In fact, let's make both&nbsp;
masses equal for simplicity.

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:09.880
Then, firing splits the big&nbsp;
mass into two smaller masses,

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:14.760
each following their own path, initially&nbsp;
differing by a fixed relative velocity.

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:18.920
Obviously, to get the most bang for the&nbsp;
buck, make the split when you can jump as

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:22.760
much energy levels as possible,&nbsp;
which is during the squeezing.

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:27.880
There are the points of highest speed along&nbsp;
each path, where kinetic energy climbs easiest.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:33.280
The final speed-up or slow-down merely by firing&nbsp;
within a potential well is the Oberth effect.

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:37.680
Now, if you start out at higher velocity,&nbsp;
the Oberth contribution shrinks.

00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:40.480
And this makes sense: passing so quickly,

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:44.720
there is not enough time to accelerate&nbsp;
much and build up greater velocity changes.

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:48.200
And so the squeezing fades&nbsp;
for higher initial speeds.

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:49.680
It's like leveling the well.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:54.040
When passing a rock instead of a&nbsp;
planet, there is less Oberth effect.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:57.120
On the other hand, taking your time is beneficial.

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:01.680
That's why the capture/escape path is&nbsp;
super sensitive to the Oberth effect.

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:04.120
That's the path that barely escapes.

00:17:04.120 --> 00:17:09.040
Nonetheless, along each path, it's always&nbsp;
beneficial to fire at the fastest point.

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:13.160
And you get an intuitive visualization&nbsp;
by shooting non-firing blobs.

00:17:13.160 --> 00:17:15.840
This makes it somewhat of&nbsp;
a squeezing detection tool.

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:21.040
Since the phase space volume doesn't change,&nbsp;
velocity-squeezing means position expansion.

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:22.600
Apply it inside the well?!

00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:25.440
Once again, most squeezing&nbsp;
is at the fastest point.

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:29.160
Now, the question is, how does it&nbsp;
translate to the two-dimensional case?

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:30.240
It's quite similar!

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:34.000
First, let's take paths that share&nbsp;
the same point of closest encounter.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:37.680
This connects them at their point of&nbsp;
highest velocity via a single burn.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:39.640
That's great for later comparison.

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:42.360
So, shooting-off blobs that have the same size at

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:46.880
each path's slowest point, you see the&nbsp;
highest stretching at the fastest point.

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:49.640
Firing here splits the energy most effectively.

00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:53.800
So, in the diagrams, I compare firing&nbsp;
at the fastest and the slowest point.

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:55.240
This is the Oberth effect.

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:59.280
Now, you can tell; among the paths,&nbsp;
the parabola is most effective.

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:02.520
And the reason simply comes down&nbsp;
to where the slowest point is.

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:04.800
On an ellipse, it is the outmost point.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:09.920
And the more you fire at the fastest point, the&nbsp;
more it moves away, and the velocity lowers.

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:16.160
Until you get a parabola with zero velocity at&nbsp;
infinity – that's the route that barely escapes.

00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:20.520
Fire more, and you get hyperbolas&nbsp;
with non-zero velocity at infinity.

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:21.440
And that's it:

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:24.160
Having non-zero velocity at the slowest points,

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:28.880
ellipses and hyperbolas give you more kinetic&nbsp;
energy when firing on their worst side.

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:30.920
This counters the Oberth effect.

00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:33.880
You can still see a huge absolute energy increase.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:37.560
But it would be huge already,&nbsp;
even without the Oberth effect.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:42.080
And, of course, arriving on a parabola,&nbsp;
the post-firing hyperbola won't have the

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:44.680
highest energy compared to hyperbolic arrivals.

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:47.200
But the Oberth effect is "most effective."

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:52.360
So, in my simulation, I fire stronger aligned&nbsp;
with the velocity vector closer to the planet.

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:55.280
And I hope millions of routes&nbsp;
show the best combination.

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:58.320
You cannot always approach close to a parabola.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:00.960
Now, back to my friend: let's get back to Moon!

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:03.120
The blob quickly explores phase space,

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:06.800
working on millions of possible&nbsp;
routes, and … I can't see anything!

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:10.040
So, just for learning, I&nbsp;
give you something better.

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:12.880
Let's sit on a Low Blue Orbit everywhere at once.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:15.760
And remembering the Oberth&nbsp;
effect, I will fire twice.

00:19:15.760 --> 00:19:18.840
One firing to get away from&nbsp;
Blue and another firing to

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:21.400
get captured by the Moon – nowhere in between.

00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:24.080
On top, I vary the initial fire strength.

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:28.000
Each of these rings are millions&nbsp;
of spaceships, colored by strength.

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:29.520
Very soon, we hit Moon.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:33.280
And here, we could make the second&nbsp;
firing to get captured by the Moon.

00:19:33.280 --> 00:19:38.800
This family of routes is the Hohmann transfer&nbsp;
for a prograde or retrograde orbit around Moon.

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:42.800
It's pretty expensive fuel-wise&nbsp;
but quite fast: it's the first hit.

00:19:42.800 --> 00:19:44.120
But let's keep going.

00:19:44.120 --> 00:19:49.160
Every time the lines intersect with Moon,&nbsp;
a firing would conclude a family of routes.

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:52.440
And there are a lot of them –&nbsp;
I cannot even show them all!

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:55.840
But there is one type of family&nbsp;
that should draw your attention.

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:57.920
These lines slowly approach Moon.

00:19:57.920 --> 00:20:02.360
Until some tracers get ballistically&nbsp;
captured by Moon without any firing.

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:06.360
Now, seeing the winner route doesn't tell&nbsp;
you why it is possible in the first place.

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:07.680
And this is really cool.

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:12.240
Let's rerun the ballistic trajectory, and&nbsp;
I want you to focus on this section here.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:13.040
You see it?

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:17.520
We shoot off on an ellipse, but instead&nbsp;
of sticking to it, we get pulled aside.

00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:21.880
Looks pretty much like the inverse of the&nbsp;
continuous firing solution we found before.

00:20:21.880 --> 00:20:23.320
But we don't fire here!

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:25.240
So where does it come from?

00:20:25.240 --> 00:20:28.640
Surprise, surprise: it's the&nbsp;
Sun – nature fires for us!

00:20:28.640 --> 00:20:31.480
To get a first overview,&nbsp;
draw the potential energy!

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:36.000
And you clearly see: close to Blue, most&nbsp;
of the local dynamics are governed by Blue.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:38.120
And the farther you go outwards, the more

00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:41.560
the collective gravitational&nbsp;
pull shifts towards the Sun.

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:44.200
That's why we don't stick to the two-body ellipse.

00:20:44.200 --> 00:20:46.240
In this first swing of the journey,

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:51.520
the Sun gets more relevant, and especially&nbsp;
the Blue-Moon interplay gets less relevant.

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:56.600
And there is a simple model that "captures"&nbsp;
this: the restricted circular three-body model.

00:20:56.600 --> 00:21:00.840
It's a great playground with already rich&nbsp;
dynamical structures that explain a lot.

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:06.200
Now, to study the motion with the Sun always in&nbsp;
the same direction, co-rotate the reference frame.

00:21:06.200 --> 00:21:08.480
This change in perspective alters the form of the

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:12.480
equations of spaceship motion when&nbsp;
written in co-rotating coordinates.

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:14.200
By how much are they altered?

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:18.760
Exactly as needed so that the generated&nbsp;
motion looks correct from "the outside."

00:21:18.760 --> 00:21:20.160
I give you an example.

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:24.360
Here, I look at a resting mass from a&nbsp;
moving and rotating reference frame.

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:28.120
From that perspective, the mass&nbsp;
seems to get pushed around.

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:32.920
So, computing in this accelerating reference&nbsp;
frame, you have to add some forces.

00:21:32.920 --> 00:21:37.800
Which combined push exactly as needed to&nbsp;
look like resting after back-transformation.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:42.480
So, these forces only pop up for kinematic&nbsp;
reasons – once we mess with the coordinates.

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:46.960
You describe the same physical motion after&nbsp;
all – just from a fancy-dancy perspective.

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:50.240
But, as it goes, some of this mess is beneficial.

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:54.080
Here, the gravitational forces keep&nbsp;
both masses on circular orbits.

00:21:54.080 --> 00:21:56.280
And focus on the smaller mass here.

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:59.280
Once co-rotating with the same rotational speed,

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:03.280
the centrifugal force makes&nbsp;
the mass apparently force-free.

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:08.200
In fact, you can compute the centrifugal&nbsp;
force anywhere by plugging in some position.

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:13.800
So, we can see it as a downhill push coming from&nbsp;
a position-based potential scaled with the mass.

00:22:13.800 --> 00:22:16.800
And we can do the same with&nbsp;
the gravitational potential.

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:19.880
Obviously, bake them into an effective potential.

00:22:19.880 --> 00:22:24.240
That's the potential the spaceship sees&nbsp;
in this co-rotating reference frame.

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:26.480
It's still pushed by the Coriolis force,

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:30.800
but the effective potential gives a hard&nbsp;
reachability limit for a given energy.

00:22:30.800 --> 00:22:36.360
And so, a non-firing blob never exceeds the&nbsp;
zero-velocity lines for a given energy level.

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:39.600
You can lock the blobs to&nbsp;
certain energetic regions.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:41.800
This potential is a great predictive tool.

00:22:41.800 --> 00:22:44.320
And you can do even more.

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:46.240
Apparently, you can sit at the stationary

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:49.200
Lagrange points to rest&nbsp;
in the co-rotating system.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:51.840
Let's isolate this point here and kick forward.

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:55.920
The Coriolis force immediately pushes&nbsp;
to the right of the relative velocity.

00:22:55.920 --> 00:22:57.440
Eventually, we drift off.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:01.080
But if we start next to the Lagrange&nbsp;
point and shoot off just right,

00:23:01.080 --> 00:23:04.560
we can balance both forces to&nbsp;
orbit around the Lagrange point.

00:23:04.560 --> 00:23:07.440
In a way, this is how my code computes this orbit.

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:12.440
The shooting method tweaks the kick-off to&nbsp;
nail down this orbit by rewarding periodicity.

00:23:12.440 --> 00:23:17.400
And for this symmetric orbit type it's enough&nbsp;
to look for perpendicular axis crossings.

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:19.760
Now, when there is one orbit, there are more.

00:23:19.760 --> 00:23:22.640
Tweaking not just the kick-off&nbsp;
velocity but the location as

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:26.440
well – meaning the whole phase space&nbsp;
point – you land on a different orbit.

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:30.480
Just enforce periodicity and some&nbsp;
distance measure towards the last orbit.

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:32.920
Then you performed 'numerical continuation'.

00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:35.160
You have a solution point of a problem - here a

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:39.080
phase space point of an orbit - and&nbsp;
you find points on neighbor orbits.

00:23:39.080 --> 00:23:43.280
Now, tracing such an orbit numerically,&nbsp;
you can check it's hard to stay on them.

00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:46.800
They are surrounded by regions in&nbsp;
phase space that, once you sit on them,

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:51.760
lead you to the orbit or away from&nbsp;
it – stable or unstable manifolds.

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:54.000
These are the structures I'm looking for.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:54.560
Why?

00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:56.760
Because they tell you in advance what you get.

00:23:56.760 --> 00:23:59.880
Take the one-dimensional spaceship&nbsp;
with a hill-shaped potential.

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:02.320
Starting anywhere makes you follow the flow.

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:04.800
Starting at the equilibrium leads you nowhere.

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:06.200
This point is not an orbit,

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:10.440
but let's pretend it is equivalent as&nbsp;
in: once you sit on it, you stay on it.

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:13.000
A stationary feature of the phase space motion.

00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:15.920
And the stable and unstable&nbsp;
manifolds are nothing more

00:24:15.920 --> 00:24:18.480
but curves that lead you to and from that point.

00:24:18.480 --> 00:24:21.840
Though you never reach it, it would&nbsp;
take an infinite amount of time.

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:24.960
But they are helpful as they tell&nbsp;
you what happens close to them.

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:29.400
You know in advance that once you ride close&nbsp;
to the stable manifold, you will curve away,

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:31.880
following either side of the unstable manifold.

00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:34.400
That's the skeleton of the phase space motion.

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:36.960
Also, taking slightly different paths is great to

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:40.400
generate some waiting time while&nbsp;
ending up at the same location.

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:43.440
Both points are great for planning a spaceflight.

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:47.880
Remember: to catch up with Moon, you need the&nbsp;
right position and velocity at the right time.

00:24:47.880 --> 00:24:50.080
And no matter how energetic you start out,

00:24:50.080 --> 00:24:53.440
you need to build up angular&nbsp;
momentum relative to Blue as well.

00:24:53.440 --> 00:24:57.040
That's why the Hohmann transfer&nbsp;
comes with a strong second burn.

00:24:57.040 --> 00:24:59.840
But the manifolds tell us how to get it for free.

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:02.720
Part of the stable manifold reaches down to Blue.

00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:07.240
You can follow close to it, having enough&nbsp;
energy but still low angular momentum.

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:11.440
But looking at the unstable manifold,&nbsp;
we know there must be the right "curve

00:25:11.440 --> 00:25:15.480
away" bringing us up here in time –&nbsp;
having much more angular momentum.

00:25:15.480 --> 00:25:19.480
Under the hood, it's the effective&nbsp;
potential and the Coriolis force pushing us.

00:25:19.480 --> 00:25:23.520
Also note: we curve away close to the&nbsp;
outside of the stable manifold tube.

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:26.000
You can guess what happens being inside the tube.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:29.880
Now, having the angular momentum,&nbsp;
we need to approach Moon just right,

00:25:29.880 --> 00:25:34.040
which doesn't even exist in the three-body&nbsp;
system where these manifolds come from.

00:25:34.040 --> 00:25:35.760
Well, closer to Blue and Moon,

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:39.720
their restricted three-body system&nbsp;
with its manifolds is more relevant.

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:44.960
So, hopping manifolds is the name of the game –&nbsp;
and we follow the stable manifold to the Moon.

00:25:44.960 --> 00:25:50.280
Now, approaching Moon statistically, some tracers&nbsp;
get temporarily captured for a number of cycles.

00:25:50.280 --> 00:25:53.520
And to get the essence of&nbsp;
this, go ahead systematically.

00:25:53.520 --> 00:25:57.271
Any passing trajectory has a point&nbsp;
of its first closest encounter.

00:25:57.271 --> 00:26:02.360
And so you start at an angle with a perpendicular&nbsp;
kick-off – here in prograde direction.

00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:06.960
If it wouldn't be for Blue, you would then&nbsp;
make an ellipse with a predefined eccentricity.

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:11.840
But since Blue spits in the soup, count the&nbsp;
number of revolutions around Moon until escape.

00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:17.120
For that, you check the Kepler energy sign of the&nbsp;
local spaceship-Moon system after each revolution.

00:26:17.120 --> 00:26:21.320
So, n-stable orbits make n revolutions&nbsp;
around Moon with a negative energy

00:26:21.320 --> 00:26:25.520
sign at the cut section – all without&nbsp;
making a full revolution around Blue.

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:27.720
That's the simulation cut-off time.

00:26:27.720 --> 00:26:30.840
Repeat this experiment for the&nbsp;
next points along the cut section,

00:26:30.840 --> 00:26:33.480
and you find changes in the n-stability.

00:26:33.480 --> 00:26:37.280
Now, let's focus on 0- and 1-stable&nbsp;
orbits and their boundaries.

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:39.480
These boundary points collected for all

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:43.640
angles for the given eccentricity&nbsp;
make the weak stability boundary.

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:47.440
And along this boundary, you find&nbsp;
points that will crash into Moon.

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:50.240
Now, these sets of points&nbsp;
are great for two reasons:

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:54.520
First, obviously, they tell you where to&nbsp;
be in phase space to get captured by Moon.

00:26:54.520 --> 00:26:57.600
This is similar to the effective&nbsp;
potential and manifold ideas.

00:26:58.160 --> 00:27:03.080
Second, while sometimes coinciding with&nbsp;
manifold points, they make a more general tool.

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:05.760
Manifolds are great; they split energetic regions,

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:08.680
making it easy to see where&nbsp;
stuff goes in and goes out.

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:10.880
But they need orbits to emerge from.

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:13.760
And the orbits need Lagrange&nbsp;
points to emerge from.

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:18.720
And these Lagrange points here need the restricted&nbsp;
circular three-body system to emerge from.

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:21.200
When you go with a restricted four-body system,

00:27:21.200 --> 00:27:24.360
the Lagrange points and the&nbsp;
emerging structures are no more.

00:27:24.360 --> 00:27:27.880
The three body sub-systems are&nbsp;
disturbed by the remaining mass.

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:31.320
And so you don't exactly rest in&nbsp;
the co-rotating reference frame.

00:27:31.320 --> 00:27:35.560
But there are still sensitive regions, and&nbsp;
the manifolds still make a great guide.

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:39.480
The weak stability boundary – you can&nbsp;
build it for more general systems directly.

00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:42.440
You simply count revolutions&nbsp;
– however, they came about.

00:27:42.440 --> 00:27:44.440
It's just time-dependent now.

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:50.080
But honestly, if nature gently carries you close&nbsp;
to Moon, fire a little bit to safely stay with it.

00:27:50.080 --> 00:27:52.320
That's how you blow up the n-stability.

00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:55.960
You jump from the 1-stability&nbsp;
for the eccentric approach to

00:27:55.960 --> 00:28:00.120
the above 10-stability for the circular orbit.

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:02.840
These are the phase space&nbsp;
structures for low-energy

00:28:02.840 --> 00:28:05.560
transfer to the Moon – or anywhere else?

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:09.000
Starting from a Low Blue&nbsp;
Orbit fire to get up here.

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:14.680
Then, follow inside the stable manifold tube and&nbsp;
slip through the orbit into the unstable manifold.

00:28:14.680 --> 00:28:18.800
If we are lucky, it overlaps with&nbsp;
the stable manifold of planet Yellow.

00:28:18.800 --> 00:28:19.640
But we aren't.

00:28:19.640 --> 00:28:21.160
They don't even come close.

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:25.840
Visualizing the speed by an upward shift,&nbsp;
you can guess the phase space distance.

00:28:25.840 --> 00:28:29.360
So, you look for a&nbsp;
cost-effective two-burn hop-over.

00:28:29.360 --> 00:28:31.680
That's an intermediate Hohmann transfer.

00:28:31.680 --> 00:28:35.440
From here on, it is the inverted&nbsp;
sequence to a Low Yellow Orbit.

00:28:35.440 --> 00:28:39.840
This sequence of manifolds is part of&nbsp;
the interplanetary transport network.

00:28:39.840 --> 00:28:44.560
Now, as fancy as it looks, it's hard to see if&nbsp;
it's better than a classical Hohmann transfer.

00:28:44.560 --> 00:28:50.080
So, let me convince you with a much more intuitive&nbsp;
Blue-Moon example that such routes can be cheaper.

00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:53.160
Remember: Since, all we got&nbsp;
to do is getting up here,

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:56.360
let's get there via gravity assist by Moon itself.

00:28:56.360 --> 00:29:00.680
The Hohmann transfer would need a strong&nbsp;
retrograde burn to get captured by Moon.

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:03.800
The low-energy ballistic transfer&nbsp;
gets you captured for free.

00:29:03.800 --> 00:29:05.280
It just takes some time.

00:29:05.280 --> 00:29:06.440
That's the trade-off.

00:29:06.440 --> 00:29:11.080
And for a fair comparison, both missions&nbsp;
lead here to a 2-stable capture.

00:29:11.080 --> 00:29:16.560
Also, the Coriolis force and effective potential&nbsp;
work in our favor in the fourth quadrant as well.

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:19.720
Here, the angular momentum keeps&nbsp;
building up in the right way.

00:29:19.720 --> 00:29:22.200
So you can expect similar routes to the Moon.

00:29:22.200 --> 00:29:24.040
You can also use inner routes.

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:27.920
That's when you hop on Blue-Moon's&nbsp;
inner stable manifold – either via

00:29:27.920 --> 00:29:30.960
a Hohmann transfer or the low thrust spiral.

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:33.040
This carries you up to the Moon.

00:29:33.040 --> 00:29:37.800
And to show what else is possible, the orbit&nbsp;
families we found are linked to other families.

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:43.160
The planar Lyapunov orbits – the ones I have shown&nbsp;
before – bifurcate into Halo orbits and many more.

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:44.920
Each having its own manifolds.

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:46.000
It's a mess.

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:47.680
It's helpful, nonetheless.

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:49.800
Things seem cluttered from time to time,

00:29:49.800 --> 00:29:53.760
leaving you wondering if you ever make&nbsp;
it: there is always a way, always!

00:29:53.760 --> 00:29:56.017
Step 8: Time to get home.

00:29:57.040 --> 00:29:58.200
This is the problem:

00:29:58.200 --> 00:30:03.080
My friend makes an elliptic orbit at planet Silk&nbsp;
with enough propellant to reach planet Blue.

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:06.080
But not enough to get into&nbsp;
a circular Low Blue Orbit.

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:07.200
Oxygen is limited.

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:10.000
And hull integrity is … we shouldn't aerobrake.

00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:13.680
So, to lose energy, we need&nbsp;
clever swing-by's, possibly fired.

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:14.760
Speaking of firing:

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:17.160
On the first elliptic passage – firing closest

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:20.840
to the planet doesn't make the best&nbsp;
departure angle relative to the Sun.

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:24.160
Not all of the Oberth speed boost&nbsp;
goes in the backwards direction.

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:27.560
That's why the best firing point&nbsp;
makes an inefficient compromise.

00:30:27.560 --> 00:30:31.640
Simply waiting gives better departure&nbsp;
angles, and you can reach "farther down."

00:30:31.640 --> 00:30:35.760
Then again, the planets must align for&nbsp;
great swing-bys – that's a phasing problem.

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:37.080
This is how I made it.

00:30:37.080 --> 00:30:41.160
I divided the space in an interplanetary&nbsp;
part and local planetary parts.

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:43.280
These control the tracer resolutions.

00:30:43.280 --> 00:30:46.600
Now, the tracers without contact&nbsp;
didn't make it to Blue in time.

00:30:46.600 --> 00:30:50.000
So, the winner route is hidden&nbsp;
in the uninvestigated shadows.

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:53.520
These I explore with higher&nbsp;
resolution sampled from the recording.

00:30:53.520 --> 00:30:56.040
Eventually, I iterate along encounters.

00:30:56.040 --> 00:31:00.680
Now, at planet Blue, some tracers have enough&nbsp;
propellant to decelerate onto the goal orbit.

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:02.200
That makes a complete route.

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:06.920
And since tiny changes make a huge difference,&nbsp;
you get infinitely many routes per family.

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:11.640
Simply pick a suitable route from an&nbsp;
efficient cluster, trading time vs. propellant.

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:16.680
This route makes five revolutions around planet&nbsp;
Silk before leaving for the inner planets.

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:19.680
Here, a swing-by at Blue&nbsp;
takes away a lot of energy.

00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:24.120
Still, it needs additional firing to&nbsp;
make the final arc hit home just right.

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:28.480
If you can invest more travel time, take&nbsp;
this route and save even more propellant.

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:31.880
The Blue-Yellow double swing-by&nbsp;
takes away even more energy.

00:31:31.880 --> 00:31:34.680
And then just wait until you catch up with Blue.

00:31:34.680 --> 00:31:38.880
Both variants are much better than the&nbsp;
instant and optimal Hohmann transfer.

00:31:38.880 --> 00:31:42.400
And as expected, since I encouraged&nbsp;
Oberth-promoting swing-bys,

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:45.840
the winners are mostly composed&nbsp;
of locally efficient maneuvers.

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:50.320
And if you color it not by cluster but&nbsp;
by interplanetary segment, you get this.

00:31:50.320 --> 00:31:55.640
The more swing-bys you consider, the easier it&nbsp;
is to get the final arc close to Blue's orbit.

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:59.560
Making it easier to arrive&nbsp;
on a low-energy manifold.

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:02.520
By the way, this is another&nbsp;
riddle I'm trying to solve.

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:05.440
Maybe you can help me with that&nbsp;
… if you enjoyed the video?

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:06.480
Thank you for watching!

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:07.702
Oberth and out.

